 :result
 "IC09 execute is 2.87x faster. Planning is flat at 1.02x -- and planning is unchanged code between these pins, so it is the load control, and it says the host did not move. IC02 is 1.14x, within noise.

  Scans are identical (885 / 1,793) on both pins, so the plan and the store traffic are unchanged. What changed is how many bindings the join carries between steps: IC09 was moving 55,335 into a step that used 8,130 distinct values of one variable, and those 47,000 existed only because two earlier variables were still attached."

 :a-regression-i-reported-and-then-unreported
 "Measuring B1+B3 against the EARLIER B1 run showed IC02 execute at 0.54x -- about twice as slow -- and I had a structural story ready for it: IC02 drops one variable at step 0 and merges nothing, so it pays select-keys and a set rebuild for no return.

  The same-window comparison says 1.14x. The regression was the earlier run's host, not the code. The story was plausible and it was about something that was not happening.

  Third time in this session that comparing a fresh measurement against an older one produced a false result, and the second time I had an explanation ready for a number that was not real. Same-window is not a refinement of the method; without it the measurement is not evidence."

 :the-guard-still-only-half-fires
 "The structural point survives even though the regression did not. `prune-bindings` skips when every variable is still needed, but not when variables ARE dropped and nothing merges -- which is IC02's step 0 exactly. That costs something; it is simply too small to see at this scale. Worth fixing only with a measurement that can see it, not on the strength of the argument."

 :where-the-time-is-now
 ["IC09: 154 ms planning, 412 ms execute, down from 627 / 1,085 before B1 and B3. Both halves roughly a third of what they were."
  "Scan-side work remains finished as a source of wins (9% of execute at last count)."
  "B2 -- pushing the limit into the join -- is the remaining large item and is not implemented. Evidence for it: IC02 matches 910 of 7,355 messages, so a date-ordered scan stopping at 20 would touch about 160 rows, and all four LDBC complex reads are most-recent-N queries."]

 :caveats
 ["Single run per pin, one start person. Counts exact; milliseconds are same-window ratios only."
  "IC02's execute is 144-165 ms and its planning 11-14 ms -- both small enough that its ratios carry little weight either way. IC09 is the query this receipt can actually speak about."]

